1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,447
Computational Skepticism, Exam Prep.

2
00:00:01,497 --> 00:00:03,319
Key Terms: The Confidence Illusion.

3
00:00:03,369 --> 00:00:07,811
Thirty-three terms for judging a confident
number, from sample space and Bayes'

4
00:00:07,861 --> 00:00:10,057
theorem to calibration and distribution
shift.

5
00:00:10,107 --> 00:00:15,297
With them you can say exactly what a
stated ninety-nine percent does and does

6
00:00:15,347 --> 00:00:16,420
not tell you.

7
00:00:17,570 --> 00:00:17,788
Hallo.

8
00:00:17,838 --> 00:00:19,394
This is Liam, in for Bear.

9
00:00:19,444 --> 00:00:22,607
Many people believe a confident number
tells you how sure to be.

10
00:00:22,657 --> 00:00:25,552
A model says ninety-nine percent, so you
trust it ninety-nine percent.

11
00:00:25,602 --> 00:00:28,229
But the stated number and what happens are
different things.

12
00:00:28,279 --> 00:00:30,906
A confident number has to be checked
against what happened.

13
00:00:30,956 --> 00:00:34,922
This film goes through thirty-three terms
for making that check, each one shown in a

14
00:00:34,972 --> 00:00:35,190
picture.

15
00:00:35,540 --> 00:00:38,512
Six labels sit in the corners of the
pictures that follow.

16
00:00:38,562 --> 00:00:41,809
In the book text: the course text uses and
explains the term.

17
00:00:41,859 --> 00:00:46,479
In the glossary: the term is on the course
term list, but the text does not explain

18
00:00:46,529 --> 00:00:46,753
it.

19
00:00:46,803 --> 00:00:49,775
Computed: the numbers are worked out on
screen from stated inputs.

20
00:00:49,825 --> 00:00:54,171
Simulation: a made-up run with a fixed
seed, so it comes out the same every time.

21
00:00:54,221 --> 00:00:56,918
Illustrative data: numbers chosen to show
the idea, not measured.

22
00:00:56,968 --> 00:00:59,940
Conceptual: a drawing of the idea, with no
data behind it.

23
00:01:00,290 --> 00:01:03,694
This is a map of thirty-three terms in
four groups.

24
00:01:03,744 --> 00:01:08,530
All of them answer one question, what does
a confident number actually tell you.

25
00:01:08,580 --> 00:01:13,021
The first group, what a probability is,
holds sample space, event, the three

26
00:01:13,071 --> 00:01:15,785
axioms, classical probability, frequentist
probability, Bayesian probability and

27
00:01:15,835 --> 00:01:16,130
prior.

28
00:01:16,480 --> 00:01:21,763
The second group is updating on evidence,
how a chance changes once you know more.

29
00:01:21,813 --> 00:01:24,963
Its terms are conditional probability, the
multiplication rule, independence,

30
00:01:25,013 --> 00:01:27,808
partition, total probability, likelihood,
posterior and Bayes' theorem.

31
00:01:27,858 --> 00:01:32,430
Each one is a step in turning new
information into a new number.

32
00:01:32,780 --> 00:01:34,989
The third group is rare events.

33
00:01:35,039 --> 00:01:39,129
Its terms are base rate, sensitivity,
specificity, false positive, and PPV,

34
00:01:39,179 --> 00:01:40,259
positive predictive value.

35
00:01:40,309 --> 00:01:45,152
Together they explain why a test that is
ninety-nine percent accurate can still

36
00:01:45,202 --> 00:01:48,540
flag mostly healthy people when the
condition is rare.

37
00:01:48,890 --> 00:01:54,282
The last group, earning the number, asks
whether stated confidence is checked, and

38
00:01:54,332 --> 00:01:55,538
when it breaks.

39
00:01:55,588 --> 00:01:58,468
Checking covers calibration, the
calibration curve, overconfidence,

40
00:01:58,518 --> 00:02:01,817
decorative number, temperature scaling,
ECE, expected calibration error,

41
00:02:01,867 --> 00:02:03,910
calibration baseline and plausibility
auditing.

42
00:02:03,960 --> 00:02:08,096
Breaking covers distribution shift, Hume's
induction problem, the central limit

43
00:02:08,146 --> 00:02:09,770
theorem, heavy-tailed and loss.

44
00:02:10,120 --> 00:02:12,595
Here are the six faces of a single die.

45
00:02:12,645 --> 00:02:17,085
That full list is the sample space, every
outcome that could happen, and one of them

46
00:02:17,135 --> 00:02:17,366
must.

47
00:02:17,416 --> 00:02:19,891
Everything else in probability is built
from this list.

48
00:02:19,941 --> 00:02:22,978
For example, when an applicant-tracking
system screens your résumé, the sample

49
00:02:23,028 --> 00:02:24,942
space is every result it can give.

50
00:02:24,992 --> 00:02:28,590
It rejects you, holds you for review, or
passes you to a recruiter.

51
00:02:28,940 --> 00:02:31,002
Now faces two, four and six turn red.

52
00:02:31,052 --> 00:02:33,907
That group is an event, the die shows an
even number.

53
00:02:33,957 --> 00:02:37,076
An event is any part of the sample space
you care about.

54
00:02:37,126 --> 00:02:39,452
Three of six equally likely faces gives
one half.

55
00:02:39,502 --> 00:02:43,677
For example, an application can end in no
reply, a rejection, a phone screen or an

56
00:02:43,727 --> 00:02:43,941
interview.

57
00:02:43,991 --> 00:02:45,789
Hearing back at all is an event.

58
00:02:45,839 --> 00:02:48,430
It groups the rejection, the phone screen
and the interview.

59
00:02:48,780 --> 00:02:52,252
A bar cut into six equal pieces shows the
three axioms.

60
00:02:52,302 --> 00:02:54,172
Nonnegativity, no piece is below zero.

61
00:02:54,222 --> 00:02:55,773
Normalization, the pieces total one.

62
00:02:55,823 --> 00:03:00,255
Additivity, outcomes that cannot happen
together add, so the red pieces sum to one

63
00:03:00,305 --> 00:03:00,575
half.

64
00:03:00,625 --> 00:03:05,057
For example, a tool gives chances for how
one application ends, no reply, rejection,

65
00:03:05,107 --> 00:03:05,697
or interview.

66
00:03:05,747 --> 00:03:10,499
None can be below zero, together they must
make one, and the chance of hearing

67
00:03:10,549 --> 00:03:12,100
anything is rejection plus interview.

68
00:03:12,450 --> 00:03:17,565
Five balls, each as likely to be drawn as
any other, with the one labelled yellow

69
00:03:17,615 --> 00:03:17,888
highlighted.

70
00:03:17,938 --> 00:03:19,180
Classical probability is counting.

71
00:03:19,230 --> 00:03:23,376
One favourable outcome out of five equally
likely outcomes gives zero point two.

72
00:03:23,426 --> 00:03:24,668
It needs that symmetry.

73
00:03:24,718 --> 00:03:28,865
For example, five finalists are equally
qualified, so a recruiter draws one name

74
00:03:28,915 --> 00:03:31,124
at random for the first interview slot.

75
00:03:31,174 --> 00:03:34,030
Counting alone gives each finalist one
chance in five.

76
00:03:34,380 --> 00:03:37,718
The red line is the share of heads as a
coin flips.

77
00:03:37,768 --> 00:03:40,259
Frequentist probability is that share over
a long run.

78
00:03:40,309 --> 00:03:44,777
In this simulated run it is zero point
four after twenty flips and near one half

79
00:03:44,827 --> 00:03:45,624
after five hundred.

80
00:03:45,674 --> 00:03:49,577
For example, a job board counts, across a
great many applications sent through it,

81
00:03:49,627 --> 00:03:51,554
the share that led to an interview.

82
00:03:51,604 --> 00:03:53,530
That long-run share is a frequentist
probability.

83
00:03:53,880 --> 00:03:56,819
Each engineer gets a grey bar and a red
bar.

84
00:03:56,869 --> 00:04:01,003
Bayesian probability is a degree of belief
that evidence updates, and the prior is

85
00:04:01,053 --> 00:04:02,497
the belief before the evidence.

86
00:04:02,547 --> 00:04:06,980
In this made-up example the same evidence
moves a prior of ten percent to thirty-one

87
00:04:07,030 --> 00:04:08,773
percent, and fifty percent to eighty.

88
00:04:08,823 --> 00:04:11,762
For example, two recruiters see the same
strong work sample.

89
00:04:11,812 --> 00:04:13,256
One began doubtful, one hopeful.

90
00:04:13,306 --> 00:04:15,049
Those starting beliefs are their priors.

91
00:04:15,099 --> 00:04:17,440
Both raise their belief, correctly, and
still differ.

92
00:04:17,790 --> 00:04:19,244
A square of all cases.

93
00:04:19,294 --> 00:04:22,253
The left block is flagged, its red part
also high-acuity.

94
00:04:22,303 --> 00:04:25,863
Conditional probability is the chance of
one thing once another is known.

95
00:04:25,913 --> 00:04:29,775
In this made-up example, red is about
forty percent of the flagged block.

96
00:04:29,825 --> 00:04:33,686
For example, the chance an applicant gets
an interview, once you know the

97
00:04:33,736 --> 00:04:36,394
application came with a referral, is a
conditional probability.

98
00:04:36,444 --> 00:04:38,500
Knowing about the referral changes the
number.

99
00:04:38,850 --> 00:04:42,474
Three bars for three calls in a chain, and
all three must work.

100
00:04:42,524 --> 00:04:45,866
The multiplication rule, also called the
chain rule, multiplies each step's chance

101
00:04:45,916 --> 00:04:47,279
given the steps before it.

102
00:04:47,329 --> 00:04:50,105
Ninety percent, then eighty-five, then
eighty, leaves about sixty-one percent.

103
00:04:50,155 --> 00:04:54,062
For example, an agent finds a listing,
tailors your résumé to it, then submits.

104
00:04:54,112 --> 00:04:58,019
If it picked the wrong listing, the
tailoring is likely wrong too, so each

105
00:04:58,069 --> 00:05:00,280
step's chance is taken given the steps
before.

106
00:05:00,630 --> 00:05:02,536
Two bars for the same three steps.

107
00:05:02,586 --> 00:05:05,329
Independence means one event tells you
nothing about the other.

108
00:05:05,379 --> 00:05:08,682
Assume it, and ninety percent three times
gives seventy-three percent, where the

109
00:05:08,732 --> 00:05:09,799
chain rule gave sixty-one.

110
00:05:09,849 --> 00:05:13,431
For example, whether you hear back from
one company tells you nothing about

111
00:05:13,481 --> 00:05:16,784
whether a stranger in another city hears
back from a different one.

112
00:05:16,834 --> 00:05:17,622
Those are independent.

113
00:05:17,672 --> 00:05:20,974
Two of your own applications sent with the
same résumé are not.

114
00:05:21,024 --> 00:05:22,930
One flaw in the résumé hurts both.

115
00:05:23,280 --> 00:05:25,718
One bar holds every case, split by model
version.

116
00:05:25,768 --> 00:05:29,313
A partition is pieces that do not overlap
and together cover every case.

117
00:05:29,363 --> 00:05:32,907
In this made-up fleet the shares are
forty, thirty and thirty percent, one

118
00:05:32,957 --> 00:05:33,736
hundred in all.

119
00:05:33,786 --> 00:05:37,884
For example, sort every application an
agent sent this month by where it went, a

120
00:05:37,934 --> 00:05:40,372
job board, a company site, or a
recruiter's inbox.

121
00:05:40,422 --> 00:05:43,690
If each went to exactly one place, those
groups are a partition.

122
00:05:44,040 --> 00:05:47,707
Each version now has a bar for its share
times its error rate.

123
00:05:47,757 --> 00:05:50,853
Total probability weights each piece's
chance by its share, then adds.

124
00:05:50,903 --> 00:05:54,856
The sum is three point two percent, an
error rate no single version reports.

125
00:05:54,906 --> 00:05:57,715
For example, a job-market report gives one
overall interview rate.

126
00:05:57,765 --> 00:06:01,432
To get it, take each job category's
interview rate, weight it by that

127
00:06:01,482 --> 00:06:03,720
category's share of applicants, and add
them up.

128
00:06:04,070 --> 00:06:05,909
Work backwards from a wrong output.

129
00:06:05,959 --> 00:06:09,688
Likelihood is the chance of the evidence
if a hypothesis is true.

130
00:06:09,738 --> 00:06:11,892
Posterior is the belief after the
evidence.

131
00:06:11,942 --> 00:06:15,986
Bayes' theorem gives it, prior times
likelihood, divided by the chance of the

132
00:06:16,036 --> 00:06:16,301
evidence.

133
00:06:16,351 --> 00:06:20,394
Version C runs thirty percent of traffic,
but makes about forty-seven percent of

134
00:06:20,444 --> 00:06:20,709
errors.

135
00:06:20,759 --> 00:06:24,802
For example, a résumé comes back rejected,
by software or a human reviewer.

136
00:06:24,852 --> 00:06:27,951
The likelihood is how often each rejects
résumés like it.

137
00:06:28,001 --> 00:06:31,730
The posterior is how strongly you now
believe each one did it.

138
00:06:32,080 --> 00:06:36,424
A grid of ten thousand squares, one per
person, with a single red square for the

139
00:06:36,474 --> 00:06:37,248
one sick person.

140
00:06:37,298 --> 00:06:40,543
The base rate is how common the thing is
before any test.

141
00:06:40,593 --> 00:06:42,465
Here it is one in ten thousand.

142
00:06:42,515 --> 00:06:46,585
For example, among everyone who applies to
a popular job posting, only a tiny share

143
00:06:46,635 --> 00:06:47,134
get hired.

144
00:06:47,184 --> 00:06:49,880
That share, before any screening runs, is
the base rate.

145
00:06:50,230 --> 00:06:53,490
Now the test runs, and black squares
appear across the grid.

146
00:06:53,540 --> 00:06:57,703
Sensitivity is the share of the truly sick
the test flags, here ninety-nine percent.

147
00:06:57,753 --> 00:07:01,013
Specificity is the share of the healthy it
clears, also ninety-nine.

148
00:07:01,063 --> 00:07:05,227
The other one percent, about one hundred
people, are flagged anyway, each a false

149
00:07:05,277 --> 00:07:05,527
positive.

150
00:07:05,577 --> 00:07:07,935
For example, a résumé screener passes
strong applicants.

151
00:07:07,985 --> 00:07:12,449
Sensitivity is the share of strong ones it
passes, specificity the share of weak ones

152
00:07:12,499 --> 00:07:13,352
it turns away.

153
00:07:13,402 --> 00:07:16,060
A weak one it passes is a false positive.

154
00:07:16,410 --> 00:07:18,821
The cleared squares fade, leaving only the
flagged.

155
00:07:18,871 --> 00:07:23,436
PPV, positive predictive value, is the
share of flags that are real, about one in

156
00:07:23,486 --> 00:07:25,897
one hundred and one, just under one
percent.

157
00:07:25,947 --> 00:07:28,666
Sensitivity starts from the sick, PPV from
the flag.

158
00:07:28,716 --> 00:07:31,434
For example, a tool flags job listings as
scams.

159
00:07:31,484 --> 00:07:34,818
PPV is the share of flagged listings that
really are scams.

160
00:07:34,868 --> 00:07:38,510
If scams are rare among listings, many
flags land on honest jobs.

161
00:07:38,860 --> 00:07:42,102
The bars are a thousand cases a model
rated seventy percent.

162
00:07:42,152 --> 00:07:45,694
Calibration means stated confidence
matches how often it comes true, so seven

163
00:07:45,744 --> 00:07:47,191
hundred should turn out positive.

164
00:07:47,241 --> 00:07:49,286
Four hundred is overconfident, nine
hundred underconfident.

165
00:07:49,336 --> 00:07:50,782
Accuracy is a different question.

166
00:07:50,832 --> 00:07:55,571
For example, an AI tool tells a group of
job seekers it is seventy percent sure

167
00:07:55,621 --> 00:07:57,068
each will get an interview.

168
00:07:57,118 --> 00:08:00,360
If the tool is calibrated, about seventy
percent of them do.

169
00:08:00,710 --> 00:08:02,580
This chart is a calibration curve.

170
00:08:02,630 --> 00:08:06,420
Stated confidence runs across, actual
accuracy runs up, and the dashed diagonal

171
00:08:06,470 --> 00:08:07,380
is perfect calibration.

172
00:08:07,430 --> 00:08:12,180
In this simulated run of two thousand five
hundred cases, the dots sag below it.

173
00:08:12,230 --> 00:08:16,020
That gap is overconfidence, stated
confidence above how often the model is

174
00:08:16,070 --> 00:08:16,340
right.

175
00:08:16,390 --> 00:08:20,500
For example, chart a fit-scoring agent's
stated chance of an interview against how

176
00:08:20,550 --> 00:08:22,100
often its users got one.

177
00:08:22,150 --> 00:08:26,580
If its top scores come true less often
than they claim, it is overconfident.

178
00:08:26,930 --> 00:08:29,955
The red line marks the gap at the top dot.

179
00:08:30,005 --> 00:08:32,722
The model says ninety-seven percent, and
eighty-five percent happens.

180
00:08:32,772 --> 00:08:36,412
A decorative number is a confident figure
nobody has checked against outcomes.

181
00:08:36,462 --> 00:08:38,565
Without this curve, that ninety-seven is
one.

182
00:08:38,615 --> 00:08:42,562
For example, a job-matching app shows a
high match score beside a listing.

183
00:08:42,612 --> 00:08:47,175
If nobody has checked how often high
scores led to interviews, that score is a

184
00:08:47,225 --> 00:08:47,790
decorative number.

185
00:08:48,140 --> 00:08:50,861
The bars are a model's scores for its
classes.

186
00:08:50,911 --> 00:08:54,864
Temperature scaling divides the logits,
the raw scores, by one fitted number, T.

187
00:08:54,914 --> 00:08:59,790
As T rises from one to two, the top score
falls from eighty-four percent to sixty-

188
00:08:59,840 --> 00:09:01,638
two, and the order never changes.

189
00:09:01,688 --> 00:09:05,025
Sampling temperature is different, it sets
how random generated text is.

190
00:09:05,075 --> 00:09:09,027
For example, a fit-scoring model is too
sure about its top job matches.

191
00:09:09,077 --> 00:09:13,030
Temperature scaling softens every score,
and the listings stay in the same order.

192
00:09:13,380 --> 00:09:15,946
Hollow dots are before scaling, red dots
after.

193
00:09:15,996 --> 00:09:19,871
ECE, expected calibration error, is the
average gap between stated and actual,

194
00:09:19,921 --> 00:09:21,179
weighted by bin size.

195
00:09:21,229 --> 00:09:26,412
In this simulated run it falls from zero
point one one to zero point zero two.

196
00:09:26,462 --> 00:09:30,664
For example, group a résumé screener's
past calls by how sure it was.

197
00:09:30,714 --> 00:09:34,915
Compare each group's stated confidence
with how often it was right, then average

198
00:09:34,965 --> 00:09:36,878
the gaps, counting bigger groups more.

199
00:09:36,928 --> 00:09:38,840
That one score is its ECE.

200
00:09:39,190 --> 00:09:42,165
Twenty ninety percent intervals, ticked if
they held the truth.

201
00:09:42,215 --> 00:09:46,097
The calibration baseline is that exercise,
write the intervals, then count the hits.

202
00:09:46,147 --> 00:09:49,727
In this simulated set ten of twenty hit,
where calibrated gives eighteen.

203
00:09:49,777 --> 00:09:54,264
Plausibility auditing is hearing the wrong
note in a number before you can prove it

204
00:09:54,314 --> 00:09:54,566
wrong.

205
00:09:54,616 --> 00:09:59,103
For example, a job seeker writes a ninety
percent salary range for each offer before

206
00:09:59,153 --> 00:10:00,918
it arrives, then counts the hits.

207
00:10:00,968 --> 00:10:05,455
And a report that an entry-level job pays
like an executive one should sound wrong

208
00:10:05,505 --> 00:10:06,060
at once.

209
00:10:06,410 --> 00:10:09,612
Ten batches from a simulated model, not a
real deployment.

210
00:10:09,662 --> 00:10:13,515
Dashed grey is stated confidence, black is
actual accuracy, both near seventy-four

211
00:10:13,565 --> 00:10:13,840
percent.

212
00:10:13,890 --> 00:10:18,067
Distribution shift is what comes next, the
world after deployment stops matching the

213
00:10:18,117 --> 00:10:18,718
training data.

214
00:10:18,768 --> 00:10:22,945
For example, a model learned who gets
interviews from résumés written before AI

215
00:10:22,995 --> 00:10:24,246
writing tools were common.

216
00:10:24,296 --> 00:10:28,799
Now many applicants polish their résumés
with AI, and the signals it learned stop

217
00:10:28,849 --> 00:10:30,100
meaning what they did.

218
00:10:30,450 --> 00:10:33,261
At the dotted red line the world moves.

219
00:10:33,311 --> 00:10:37,194
Stated confidence stays near seventy-four
percent, while actual accuracy drops to

220
00:10:37,244 --> 00:10:37,909
about sixty-three.

221
00:10:37,959 --> 00:10:39,339
That is distribution shift.

222
00:10:39,389 --> 00:10:43,630
The outputs alone look unchanged, so you
have to watch the outcomes.

223
00:10:43,980 --> 00:10:47,248
The shading splits the past from what the
track record never saw.

224
00:10:47,298 --> 00:10:50,289
Hume's induction problem says the past
does not guarantee the future.

225
00:10:50,339 --> 00:10:53,331
The world has to cooperate, so ask what
must stay true.

226
00:10:53,381 --> 00:10:57,478
For example, a résumé tip worked in every
job search a career coach can remember.

227
00:10:57,528 --> 00:11:00,519
That record alone cannot promise it works
in the next one.

228
00:11:00,569 --> 00:11:04,390
Ask what about hiring must stay the same
for the tip to keep working.

229
00:11:04,740 --> 00:11:09,082
A running average of two thousand
simulated draws from a Gaussian, the bell

230
00:11:09,132 --> 00:11:10,434
curve, settles near zero.

231
00:11:10,484 --> 00:11:14,150
The central limit theorem says averages of
many independent, finite-variance values

232
00:11:14,200 --> 00:11:14,826
look bell-shaped.

233
00:11:14,876 --> 00:11:17,529
It needs both conditions, independence and
finite variance.

234
00:11:17,579 --> 00:11:21,922
For example, survey many large random
groups of job seekers and average each

235
00:11:21,972 --> 00:11:23,949
group's weeks to a first interview.

236
00:11:23,999 --> 00:11:29,017
Those group averages pile into a bell
shape, as long as no one's wait affects

237
00:11:29,067 --> 00:11:31,720
another's and the waits have no wild
extremes.

238
00:11:32,070 --> 00:11:36,368
The red line is the same running average
for a Cauchy distribution, and it never

239
00:11:36,418 --> 00:11:36,658
settles.

240
00:11:36,708 --> 00:11:40,136
Heavy-tailed means extremes are common
enough that the average does not converge.

241
00:11:40,186 --> 00:11:43,325
One late draw moved the average by two
point six one.

242
00:11:43,375 --> 00:11:46,513
Picture averaging the value of job offers
that include startup stock.

243
00:11:46,563 --> 00:11:51,441
Most are modest, but a rare one can be
enormous, so a single new offer can jump

244
00:11:51,491 --> 00:11:53,180
the average however many came before.

245
00:11:53,530 --> 00:11:56,132
A grid of one thousand wrong outputs, with
made-up costs.

246
00:11:56,182 --> 00:11:59,050
Loss is what being wrong costs, not how
often it happens.

247
00:11:59,100 --> 00:12:01,703
Most cost ten dollars, the red one five
hundred thousand.

248
00:12:01,753 --> 00:12:03,825
That one is ninety-eight percent of the
loss.

249
00:12:03,875 --> 00:12:07,803
For example, an agent that sends
applications for you can make a typo in a

250
00:12:07,853 --> 00:12:09,129
cover letter, which costs little.

251
00:12:09,179 --> 00:12:13,108
Or it can send your résumé to your current
employer's recruiter, which could cost you

252
00:12:13,158 --> 00:12:13,639
your job.

253
00:12:13,689 --> 00:12:15,230
Loss counts what each error costs.

254
00:12:15,580 --> 00:12:19,293
Now the look-alike pairs, each shown as
bars side by side.

255
00:12:19,343 --> 00:12:21,687
First, sensitivity against PPV, positive
predictive value.

256
00:12:21,737 --> 00:12:25,450
Sensitivity is how often the test flags
the sick, ninety-nine percent.

257
00:12:25,500 --> 00:12:31,950
PPV is how often a flag is real, under one
percent at a base rate of one in ten

258
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:32,292
thousand.

259
00:12:32,342 --> 00:12:33,660
Same test, opposite question.

260
00:12:34,010 --> 00:12:36,353
The second pair is accuracy and
calibration.

261
00:12:36,403 --> 00:12:41,480
Accuracy is how often the model is right,
in this made-up example ninety of one

262
00:12:41,530 --> 00:12:41,822
hundred.

263
00:12:41,872 --> 00:12:44,215
Calibration asks whether stated confidence
matches that.

264
00:12:44,265 --> 00:12:49,001
Here the model says ninety-nine percent
and ninety percent happens, a gap of nine

265
00:12:49,051 --> 00:12:49,343
points.

266
00:12:49,393 --> 00:12:50,710
Accurate, yet not calibrated.

267
00:12:51,060 --> 00:12:53,867
The third pair is independence and the
chain rule.

268
00:12:53,917 --> 00:12:57,677
Independence assumes the steps do not
affect one another, so ninety percent

269
00:12:57,727 --> 00:12:59,582
three times gives about seventy-three
percent.

270
00:12:59,632 --> 00:13:04,345
The chain rule takes each step's chance
given the steps before it, and gives about

271
00:13:04,395 --> 00:13:04,980
sixty-one percent.

272
00:13:05,030 --> 00:13:07,520
The gap is the cost of the assumption.

273
00:13:07,870 --> 00:13:12,102
The last pair is average loss and worst-
case loss, with illustrative costs.

274
00:13:12,152 --> 00:13:16,383
Average loss is what an error costs
averaged over many, five hundred ten

275
00:13:16,433 --> 00:13:17,701
dollars, barely a sliver.

276
00:13:17,751 --> 00:13:21,983
Worst-case loss is what the one bad error
costs, five hundred thousand dollars.

277
00:13:22,033 --> 00:13:23,300
The average hides it.

278
00:13:23,650 --> 00:13:28,897
On the left is a vague claim with an exact
instruction under it, on the right what

279
00:13:28,947 --> 00:13:29,831
the instruction gives.

280
00:13:29,881 --> 00:13:32,324
The claim, the test is ninety-nine percent
accurate.

281
00:13:32,374 --> 00:13:36,374
The instruction, compute the chance of
sick given positive from the base rate,

282
00:13:36,424 --> 00:13:37,309
sensitivity and specificity.

283
00:13:37,359 --> 00:13:39,490
The answer, about one in one hundred.

284
00:13:39,840 --> 00:13:42,294
Second claim, the agent said task
complete.

285
00:13:42,344 --> 00:13:46,943
The exact instruction is to multiply the
conditionals, each call's chance given the

286
00:13:46,993 --> 00:13:48,017
calls before it.

287
00:13:48,067 --> 00:13:53,024
On these example numbers, one report of
complete is a sixty-one percent chain, not

288
00:13:53,074 --> 00:13:55,170
the seventy-three percent independence
would give.

289
00:13:55,520 --> 00:13:58,238
Third claim, the model is ninety-four
percent confident.

290
00:13:58,288 --> 00:14:02,736
The instruction is a calibration check,
bin the cases by stated confidence on

291
00:14:02,786 --> 00:14:06,196
deployment data and compare each bin with
its actual rate.

292
00:14:06,246 --> 00:14:10,694
In the simulated run the top bin states
ninety-seven percent and delivers eighty-

293
00:14:10,744 --> 00:14:11,040
five.

294
00:14:11,390 --> 00:14:13,117
Fourth claim, fix the overconfidence.

295
00:14:13,167 --> 00:14:15,250
The exact instruction is temperature
scaling.

296
00:14:15,300 --> 00:14:20,582
Fit one temperature, T, on held-out data,
divide the logits by it, and report ECE

297
00:14:20,632 --> 00:14:21,648
before and after.

298
00:14:21,698 --> 00:14:26,625
In the simulated run ECE falls eighty-
three percent, and the ranking does not

299
00:14:26,675 --> 00:14:26,980
move.

300
00:14:27,330 --> 00:14:29,336
Last claim, average loss looks fine.

301
00:14:29,386 --> 00:14:32,419
The exact instruction is to report the
worst case.

302
00:14:32,469 --> 00:14:36,873
With the illustrative costs, one error in
a thousand, costing five hundred thousand

303
00:14:36,923 --> 00:14:39,272
dollars, is ninety-eight percent of the
loss.

304
00:14:39,322 --> 00:14:41,670
Average loss hides the one that matters.

305
00:14:42,020 --> 00:14:45,710
A checklist of six questions to ask before
you trust a confident number.

306
00:14:45,760 --> 00:14:48,299
The first is ticked, what is the base
rate.

307
00:14:48,349 --> 00:14:50,601
How common was the thing before any test.

308
00:14:50,651 --> 00:14:53,190
When it is rare, most flags are false
positives.

309
00:14:53,540 --> 00:14:57,425
Second tick, is the model calibrated on
data from this deployment.

310
00:14:57,475 --> 00:14:59,929
Calibration measured somewhere else does
not count.

311
00:14:59,979 --> 00:15:04,580
You want the calibration curve drawn from
the cases this system actually handles.

312
00:15:04,930 --> 00:15:07,944
Third tick, what is the cost distribution
of being wrong.

313
00:15:07,994 --> 00:15:12,234
Ask whether errors cost about the same, or
whether a rare one is catastrophic.

314
00:15:12,284 --> 00:15:16,830
If the loss is heavy-tailed, average loss
will look fine, so report the worst case.

315
00:15:17,180 --> 00:15:19,099
Fourth tick, what changed since training.

316
00:15:19,149 --> 00:15:23,038
This is the distribution shift question,
and behind it Hume's induction problem.

317
00:15:23,088 --> 00:15:25,008
A track record describes the past.

318
00:15:25,058 --> 00:15:28,290
Ask what must stay true for it to keep
holding.

319
00:15:28,640 --> 00:15:31,718
Fifth tick, did I multiply conditionals,
or assume independence.

320
00:15:31,768 --> 00:15:35,889
For a chain of steps, apply the
multiplication rule, each step's chance

321
00:15:35,939 --> 00:15:37,627
given the steps before it.

322
00:15:37,677 --> 00:15:41,450
Assuming independence makes the chain look
more reliable than it is.

323
00:15:41,800 --> 00:15:46,615
Last tick, what result would make me
reject this number, stated before I look.

324
00:15:46,665 --> 00:15:48,005
Then say it exactly.

325
00:15:48,055 --> 00:15:52,175
Not ninety-four percent confident, but
zero point nine seven stated, zero point

326
00:15:52,225 --> 00:15:54,260
eight five actual in that bin.

327
00:15:55,110 --> 00:15:55,702
Let's recap.

328
00:15:55,752 --> 00:16:00,196
A probability can be a count, a long-run
frequency, or a belief that updates.

329
00:16:00,246 --> 00:16:03,084
A chain multiplies conditionals, and
assuming independence overstates it.

330
00:16:03,134 --> 00:16:06,615
Bayes' theorem turns a prior and a
likelihood into a posterior.

331
00:16:06,665 --> 00:16:11,430
When the base rate is low, most flags from
a good test are false positives.

332
00:16:11,480 --> 00:16:14,318
Calibration, unlike accuracy, asks whether
stated confidence matches outcomes.

333
00:16:14,368 --> 00:16:18,170
Distribution shift and heavy-tailed loss
can break a number that once held.

334
00:16:18,520 --> 00:16:21,695
Quiz me on the terms for judging a
confident number.

335
00:16:21,745 --> 00:16:24,276
Give me one short case at a time.

336
00:16:24,326 --> 00:16:28,791
I must name the exact term, choosing from
base rate, sensitivity, PPV, which is

337
00:16:28,841 --> 00:16:31,049
positive predictive value, calibration,
overconfidence, decorative number,

338
00:16:31,099 --> 00:16:33,629
independence, the multiplication rule,
distribution shift and heavy-tailed.

339
00:16:33,679 --> 00:16:36,532
I must also say why its look-alike is
wrong.

340
00:16:36,582 --> 00:16:40,080
Hold back the answer until I reply, then
correct me briefly.

341
00:16:40,430 --> 00:16:42,602
Key Terms: The Confidence Illusion.

342
00:16:42,652 --> 00:16:44,380
At Nik Bear Brown.
